<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italian Enlightenment</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Illuminismo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> was the eighteenth-century movement of reform-minded thinkers in the Italian states, centred especially on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-verri">Pietro Verri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-genovesi">Antonio Genovesi</a> and Gaetano Filangieri applied reason to economics, law and government, attacking torture, feudal privilege and clerical power. Their ideas informed the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and Tuscany and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> Naples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-enlightenment.jpg" alt="Italian Enlightenment" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Perego · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riunione_dell%27Accademia_dei_Pugni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The movement flourished above all in the second half of the 18th century and turned on the epistemological, ethical and political questions of the age. It had local forerunners, chief among them the Neapolitan philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giambattista-vico">Giambattista Vico</a>, who departed in many ways from later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> themes but was a model for many thinkers, especially in his own city. In both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> the reformers took public office and worked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> administrations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-genovesi">Antonio Genovesi</a>, Ferdinando Galiani and Gaetano Filangieri were active under the tolerant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> king Charles VII. Genovesi founded an economic school, followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> by Ferrante de Gemmis and the economist Giuseppe Palmieri, and Filangieri's science of legislation inspired the architects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. The Real Albergo dei Poveri, built from 1751 for Charles VII, is counted a typical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> building. The Neapolitan movement long remained mostly theoretical; its last figures, among them Mario Pagano, Ignazio Ciaia and Domenico Cirillo, joined the Neapolitan Republic and were executed on 29 October 1799 after the restoration of Bourbon power.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> the movement sought practical solutions. It began at the aristocratic Accademia dei Trasformati, founded in 1743, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-verri">Pietro Verri</a> broke away with his brother Alessandro to found the Accademia dei Pugni in 1761, connected with the journal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/il-caffe">Il Caffè</a>. Its members included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a>, whose treatise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-crimes-and-punishments">Dei delitti e delle pene</a> called for the abolition of torture and the death penalty and was admired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>; in 1786 Peter Leopold abolished both in the Grand Duchy of Tuscany.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The new ideas reached poetry and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>: Giuseppe Parini satirised the nobility in his poem Il Giorno, and playwrights such as Vittorio Alfieri and Carlo Goldoni took up new themes. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> the Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> and her son Joseph II reformed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-pavia">University of Pavia</a>, bringing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-volta">Alessandro Volta</a>, Lazzaro Spallanzani and other professors of continental fame. In 1777 Maria Pellegrina Amoretti graduated there, the first woman to take a law degree at an Italian university.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Italian Enlightenment” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century Austrian Lombardy became a centre of the <b>Italian Enlightenment</b>: Pietro Verri’s journal Il Caffè and Cesare Beccaria’s On Crimes and Punishments (1764) influenced penal reform across Europe, and the Teatro alla Scala opened in 1778.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Scottish Enlightenment flourished in Edinburgh, Glasgow and Aberdeen, the <b>Italian Enlightenment</b> in Milan and Naples, and in Prussia Kant published his Critique of Pure Reason in 1781.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
